API::Docker::Role::Filters (karr #24) normalises the SHAPE of `filters` --
wraps a bare value, stringifies numbers, translates JSON booleans, croaks on
a ref or an empty value. It deliberately does NOT check filter NAMES.

The decision and the evidence, so this is not re-litigated from scratch:

A client-side name table would need one accepted set per endpoint, and the
daemon already has them. Measured against rootless Podman 5.4.2 (API 1.41)
on 2026-08-27, an unknown filter name is a hard 500, not a silent no-match:

  GET /containers/json  {"bogusname":["x"]}  500 bogusname is an invalid filter
  GET /images/json      {"danglin":["true"]} 500 invalid image filter "danglin":
                                                 must be in the format
                                                 "filter=value or filter!=value"
  GET /volumes          {"bogusname":["x"]}  500 "bogusname" is an invalid volume filter
  GET /networks         {"bogusname":["x"]}  500 invalid filter "bogusname"
  GET /events           {"bogusname":["x"]}  500 failed to parse event filters:
                                                 bogusname is an invalid filter

and Docker validates GET /plugins the same way (acceptedPluginFilterTags =
{enabled, capability}, checked by pluginFilters.Validate before List runs) --
which is how the published reference's documented `enable` turns out to be a
failed request rather than an empty list. Podman does not serve /plugins at
all: every path in the family answers 404.

So the loud case is already loud on both engines, and a client-side table
buys nothing except a new failure mode: the first time it lags the daemon it
refuses a filter the daemon would have accepted. That is strictly worse than
what it prevents.

What is genuinely silent, and what no name table would catch either:
  {"label":["1"]}       200, empty list -- a well-formed filter matching nothing
  {"reference":["nope"]} 200, empty list -- likewise
  {"dangling":["yes"]}  500 non-boolean value ... strconv.ParseBool: parsing "yes"

Reopen this only with evidence of an endpoint that accepts an unknown filter
name silently AND where the client can know the accepted set without going
stale. Documenting the accepted names per method in POD (already done for
Plugins, Secrets, Configs, Containers, Images, Networks, Volumes as part of
#24) is the cheaper half of this and is finished.
